= Scientific method — Technical =

== No universal algorithm ==
Attempts to reduce science to a fixed sequence such as observation → hypothesis → experiment → conclusion fail to describe large parts of actual scientific practice.

Methodological structure is better represented as interacting operations:

<pre>
problem formulation
↔ observation / measurement
↔ modeling
↔ hypothesis generation
↔ prediction
↔ experiment / intervention
↔ statistical or causal inference
↔ criticism / replication
↔ theory revision
</pre>

Transitions can occur in multiple directions.

== Hypothetico-deductive testing ==
A simplified structure is:

<pre>
T ∧ A ∧ I → P
</pre>

where:
* <code>T</code> = theory/hypothesis;
* <code>A</code> = auxiliary assumptions;
* <code>I</code> = initial/boundary conditions;
* <code>P</code> = predicted observation.

Observed <code>¬P</code> refutes the conjunction, not logically T alone.

This motivates methodological judgment about auxiliaries and measurement.

== Induction ==
Inductive inference extends beyond observed cases.

It can support:
* parameter estimation;
* generalization from samples;
* pattern discovery;
* forecasting;
* empirical laws.

Induction is ampliative: its conclusions contain information not deductively guaranteed by the premises.

== Abduction ==
Abductive reasoning compares candidate explanations.

Criteria can include:
* explanatory scope;
* simplicity;
* mechanism;
* fit with background knowledge;
* predictive success;
* unification;
* robustness.

These criteria are not mechanically decisive and can conflict.

== Experiment design ==
Experimental inference depends on:
* treatment assignment;
* controls;
* blinding;
* randomization;
* sampling;
* measurement validity;
* power;
* preregistration where appropriate;
* multiplicity handling;
* missing data;
* causal assumptions.

Design choices determine what inferences the data can support.

== Exploratory and confirmatory work ==
Exploratory analysis searches for patterns and hypotheses.

Confirmatory analysis evaluates pre-specified or independently motivated claims.

The distinction is epistemically important because using the same data to generate and test a hypothesis can inflate apparent evidential strength unless the selection process is modeled.

== Robustness ==
A result is more compelling when it survives:
* alternative specifications;
* independent methods;
* new data;
* sensitivity analysis;
* different instruments;
* different investigators.

Robustness is not absolute truth; it shows reduced dependence on particular methodological choices.

